EldenNet, round R1 (package @LABEL@)
@UNDERLINE@

This package runs the in-game round of EldenNet's derivation (EldenNet's decision O89) on your own PC. R0c, the
offline round you ran twice, read the game's executable file. R1 starts the game with EldenNet loaded, checks what R0c
found against the game as it runs, and then, only when you press a key at the title screen, makes EldenNet's one call
into the game: it asks the game to run a small task of EldenNet's every frame. For about ten minutes of play that task
counts and measures, and two reports of numbers come back to us. Nothing in this package is for playing with yet.

Built from EldenNet's engine branch at commit @COMMIT@ with clang-cl (Release). Never shared: a test build for you
alone, labelled @LABEL@.


How long it takes
-----------------

About 30 minutes: 5 to unzip and start, 1 to 3 at the title screen, 10 to play, a few to quit and send the files.


What is in it
-------------

  eldennet-launcher.exe   EldenNet's launcher: backs up your saves, starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat,
                          loads EldenNet, and watches the game until it ends.
  eldennet.dll            EldenNet in the game: the probe build, which runs R1 and nothing else.
  eldennet-engine.exe     EldenNet's command-line tool, here to check the reports (its lint command).
  gal\                    What R0c found, which R1 checks: the build's fingerprint (builds.toml), the singletons'
                          code shape (patterns.toml, with its note in derivations\), the task group's place
                          (layouts\2.7.2.0-ww.toml), and what to look for (leads.toml). Numbers and names of the
                          design's own, nothing taken from the game.
  README-R1.txt           This file.
  SHA256SUMS              The SHA-256 of every other file in this package.
  LICENSE.txt             EldenNet's licence (MIT).
  THIRD_PARTY.md          What EldenNet is built with, and its credits.
  THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt The licences of the libraries inside the programs.


Before you start
----------------

  - Close the game, and start Steam.
  - Unzip this package into a folder of its own, anywhere but the game's folder.
  - The launcher backs up your saves before it starts the game (in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\save-backups), and R1 does not
    run without that backup. If you want a copy of your own as well, copy the folder %APPDATA%\EldenRing somewhere
    first.
  - The game will run offline, without Easy Anti-Cheat. Use a save you are happy to play offline, and never take a save
    played with mods into online play.
  - The launcher's messages appear in a console window behind the game. Setting the game to windowed mode first makes
    them easy to read; otherwise press Alt+Tab to look, and Alt+Tab back.
  - You will press the Pause key once. On a laptop without one it is often Fn+B, Fn+P or Ctrl+Fn+B; Windows'
    On-Screen Keyboard (osk) has one too.
  - Windows or your security software may warn about an unsigned program. Allow it only if the SHA-256 values match
    (below); otherwise stop and tell us.


The steps
---------

1. Open a command prompt in this folder (in Explorer, type cmd in the address bar and press Enter), then run:

     eldennet-launcher.exe --probe

   In PowerShell (Windows Terminal's default), put .\ in front: .\eldennet-launcher.exe --probe

   The first time, it asks where eldenring.exe is if it cannot find it through Steam, and shows its notices.

2. The game starts. At the title screen, do NOT load a save. Wait. The console says, in lines starting with
   "EldenNet R1:", that it is reading the game and checking it. This usually takes under a minute, at most about
   three.

3. When the console says "The checks passed. At the title screen, with no save loaded, press Pause once.", press
   Pause once, still at the title screen.

4. Within a few seconds the console says "Task confirmed." Then, and only then, load your save.

   If it says anything else instead (a check did not pass, the call did not complete, or no confirmation after 30
   seconds), do not load a save: quit the game from the title screen and go to step 7. That is a useful result too.

5. Play for about ten minutes: go near NPCs and enemies, rest at a grace, and fast travel once (the loading screen
   matters to one of the measurements).

6. Quit the game as you usually do. The console then says whether both reports passed their check, and the launcher
   ends.

7. Send these files back to us:

     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\r1-derive.json      required
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\r1-probe.json       required
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\logs\eldennet.log         helpful
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\logs\launcher.log         helpful
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\crashes\                  the newest file there, only if the game crashed

   (Type %APPDATA%\EldenNet into Explorer's address bar to open the folder.) You can check the two reports yourself:

     eldennet-engine.exe lint --report "%APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\r1-derive.json"
     eldennet-engine.exe lint --report "%APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\r1-probe.json"

   Each must say "lint: the report is clean". The launcher has checked them the same way already.


What it does
------------

  - Backs up your saves (it only copies them), checks that Steam is running and that the game is not, and checks that
    your game is the build R0c read (2.7.2.0, worldwide). If the launcher says the build is not supported, stop: the
    game has been updated since R0c, and R0 must run again first.
  - Starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat, by writing the game's Steam app id into steam_appid.txt beside
    the game while it starts, and removing it again (eldennet-launcher.exe --cleanup removes it if a crash left it).
  - Loads eldennet.dll into the game. In the game, EldenNet reads the game's code and data as they are loaded,
    read-only and through guarded copies, and repeats R0c's derivation on them. It makes its one call into the game
    only when every check has passed and you have pressed Pause: it asks the game to run EldenNet's task, a small
    object of ours, every frame. That task then counts how the game calls it, follows the game's own task list to
    learn the task's shape, and watches a few numbers rise and stand still (the play time) to find where they live.
  - Before the call it reads the code that the call will run, and the code that code calls in turn, and summarises
    them. Just before the call it copies, into EldenNet's own memory, the part of the game's memory that the game's
    task list leads to, and twice after the call it compares that part again, counting the numbers that changed and
    saying only what kind each is (zero, a small count, a pointer and to what kind of memory). Twice after the call it
    also looks through all of the game's memory, read-only and for at most 30 seconds each time, for where the game
    kept EldenNet's task or the parts of it; it reports only counts and the kinds of memory it found them in, never
    what the memory holds.
  - Every two seconds it writes the two reports into %APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe. They hold only numbers (offsets,
    sizes, counts) and our own labels: no names, no text, no paths, nothing of your character or your PC.
  - Watches the game until it ends, and keeps a short record if it ends badly: the error's code, which module it was
    in and the time, never a memory dump.


What it never does
------------------

  - It never goes online, and the game cannot reach FromSoftware's servers without Easy Anti-Cheat.
  - It never changes your saves or the game's files (steam_appid.txt aside, which it removes).
  - It never changes the game's code or data: it reads them. Its one request at your Pause adds EldenNet's task to
    the game's own list of tasks, and it never makes that request on its own, at another moment, or twice.
  - It never runs anything else, uses the network, keeps keys or sends anything anywhere. You send the files yourself.


If the game crashes or freezes
------------------------------

  - Your saves were backed up before the game started: each backup is a folder in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\save-backups,
    named by date and time, with a manifest of the files' sizes and hashes. The game's own save is not touched by
    EldenNet; only if the game itself reports a damaged save, copy ER0000.sl2 (and ER0000.sl2.bak) from the newest
    backup back into %APPDATA%\EldenRing\<the same number>\, with the game closed.
  - If the game freezes, close it with Alt+F4, or end eldenring.exe in Task Manager.
  - Send the files of step 7, the newest crash record included. The next start of the launcher explains it once.
  - To play normally again, start the game from Steam as usual. Nothing of EldenNet stays loaded.


Check the files
---------------

In PowerShell, in this folder:

  Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 eldennet-launcher.exe, eldennet.dll, eldennet-engine.exe

Each value must equal its line in SHA256SUMS (letter case aside).


Safe use, and what we condemn
-----------------------------

The devkit's own words, as "eldennet-engine.exe probe-exe --help" prints them:

